I still think you're just to much fixating on the name.

> Please, no retorts of "do it yourself then!", because I'll ignore them
> as usual.  The argument is invalid.  I'm not a developer, but there is
> obviously much development talent and will in the community, and it's
> not being focused to move the game forward with actual releases. There
> is too much coziness with branchitis,

"Branchitis", nice.

> and with the philosophy that a
> good product can only be developed by a small, secretive team that
> publishes work at a snail's pace.  It doesn't have to be that way.

My impression is that you think it would be beneficial if a all coding
and work could be channeled into one direction. But this is not
necessarily so.

First it is really hard to do. Even on commercial products this
doesn't always work and in an OSS environment it is next to
impossible.

Second forking is not considered harmful. From Slash'Em came a lot of
things that are now in vanilla NetHack. SporkHack's Christmas trees
gave me the idea to do the Advent calendar patch. The DevNull
tournament sources have some patches that make NetHack fun to play
even for those who are not able to compete in the Trophies.

> 5 years, people.

That is nothing. I play games that haven't been changed since decades!

You might want to give the developer of Chess or Go a kick in the nuts
to finally release a new version!

